# Stem Agent — Cursor Context

## What this project is
A research project for JetBrains AI Engineering internship.
We are building a "stem agent" — one that receives a task class,
self-configures, specializes through feedback, and knows when to stop.
Domain: Python code review. Research question: does RL-style
outcome-based specialization generalize OOD better than SFT-style
demonstration-based specialization?

## What is already built — DO NOT touch these
- src/verifier/          Multi-signal verifier: pylint + AST + execution + shaped reward
- src/curriculum/        Difficulty scorer + dataset builder (30 training + 20 held-out files)
- src/stem/              LLM client, StemAgent, SkillLibrary, PromptManager
- src/specialization/    LangGraph subgraphs: SFT and RL conditions
- data/                  All Python bug files + ground_truth.json — generated, do not edit
- tests/unit/            All unit tests — green, do not break them

## Architecture — components and their roles
src/verifier/pipeline.py       → run_all_verifiers(code, ground_truth) → VerifierOutput
src/verifier/shaped_reward.py  → compute_reward() → float [0.0, 1.0]
src/curriculum/difficulty_scorer.py → score_directory() → list[ScoredFile] sorted easy→hard
src/stem/agent.py              → StemAgent: theorize() → configure() → estimate_uncertainty()
src/stem/skill_library.py      → Voyager-inspired skill store, Bayesian confidence updates
src/stem/prompt_manager.py     → EWC-inspired rollback, CI-based stopping, version history
src/specialization/graphs.py   → build_outer_graph(), build_sft_subgraph(), build_rl_subgraph()
src/specialization/runner.py   → ExperimentRunner: run(), resume(), get_state_at_iteration()
src/prompts/                   → All LLM prompts used by the agent — load from here, not hardcoded

## LangGraph architecture
Outer graph: routes to SFT subgraph or RL subgraph based on condition field.
Both subgraphs feed into evaluate_final_node.
SqliteSaver checkpointing at experiments/checkpoints.db.
Send API used for parallel variant generation (3 variants per iteration).
MemorySaver used in all tests — never SqliteSaver in tests.

## Model routing
agent_review:  gpt-4o-mini  (high volume, structured JSON, cheap)
gradient:      gpt-4o       (needs reasoning quality)
variant_gen:   gpt-4o       (needs creativity)
ewc_merge:     gpt-4o       (needs precision)
stem_phase:    gpt-4o       (foundation, runs once)

## Key design decisions (grounded in papers)
- Shaped reward (not binary): Chu et al. 2025, arXiv:2501.17161
- Verbal gradient: TextGrad, arXiv:2406.07496
- Diverse variants (not temperature sampling): Vijayakumar 2018
- Simulated annealing temperatures: Kirkpatrick 1983
- EWC rollback: Kirkpatrick et al. 2017
- Failure memory: AgenTracer, arXiv:2509.03312
- Batched evaluation: context pollution prevention, KAMI arXiv:2512.07497
- Skill library: Voyager, arXiv:2305.16291
- Constitutional critique (SFT): Bai et al. 2022, arXiv:2212.08073
- Curriculum ordering: Bengio et al. 2009

## Prompts
All LLM prompts live in src/prompts/ as .md files with {placeholder} variables.
Load them at runtime: Path("src/prompts/stem_phase/theorize.md").read_text()
Never hardcode prompt strings in Python files.
Each prompt file includes: the prompt text, expected JSON schema, paper citation.

## Data layout
data/training_bugs/    30 files, 5 pylint-detectable bug types × 6 each
data/training_clean/   same 30 files, bugs fixed
data/held_out_bugs/    20 files, 4 logic-error bug types — ZERO pylint issues
data/held_out_clean/   same 20 files, bugs fixed
data/validation/       6 files — used ONLY for tree search node evaluation
data/ground_truth.json list of GroundTruth dicts, one per buggy file

## What is NOT built yet
- src/evaluation/      Metrics: precision/recall/F1, generalization gap, Pareto, calibration
- experiments/         Experiment runner scripts, results storage
- src/logging/         Rich colorful structured logs (planned for Component 7)
- writeup/             4-page research write-up (Component 8)

## Code quality rules — always apply
- Type hints on every function
- structlog for all logging, never print()
- Dataclasses for return types, never bare dicts
- No magic numbers — constants at module top
- Every function has a one-line docstring minimum
- Comments explain WHY not WHAT
- MemorySaver in all tests, never SqliteSaver
- Zero real LLM API calls in unit tests — always mock

## What to never do
- Never modify src/verifier/, src/curriculum/, src/stem/ unless explicitly told
- Never write free-form text returns from LLM calls — always structured JSON
- Never hardcode prompt strings in Python — always load from src/prompts/
- Never use print() — always structlog
- Never use bare dicts as return types — always dataclasses
- Never add optional features not in the spec without asking first
